TITLE:: PltDistribution
summary:: A sampled histogram with the density it should have drawn over it
categories:: Libraries>PlotLib
related:: Overviews/PlotLib, Classes/PltHistogram, Classes/Pwhite

DESCRIPTION::
A link::Classes/PltHistogram:: with the analytic density on top, so "this looks
exponential" becomes "this is, or is not, exponential", with a number in the
caption saying how far off it is.

The density may be any Function of x and need not be normalised: it is normalised
numerically over the plotted range, which is why a Beta shape works here without a
Gamma function anywhere.

note::
code::\logUniform:: is SuperCollider's code::exprand::, whose density falls as
code::1/x::. It is strong::not:: the exponential distribution, whatever the name
suggests, and this is the clearest place to see it: exprand sits on 1/x to within
0.3% and misses a flat density by an order of magnitude more.
::

note::
The plotted range must be the distribution's own support. Plotting
code::exprand(0.01, 1):: over code::[0, 1]:: asks what the density does below 0.01,
where no sample can land, and the end bins report nonsense.
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
argument:: values
argument:: lo
argument:: hi
argument:: bins
argument:: pdf
A Symbol naming a density, or a Function of x.
argument:: title
argument:: width
argument:: height

METHOD:: density
A named density as an unnormalised Function: code::\uniform::,
code::\logUniform::, code::\exponential:: (lambda), code::\gauss:: (mean, dev),
code::\beta:: (a, b), code::\triangular::.
code::
PltDistribution.density(\gauss, [0.5, 0.1]);
::

METHOD:: expectedCounts
Expected counts per bin, normalised to the sample total.
argument:: pdfFunc
argument:: lo
argument:: hi
argument:: bins
argument:: total
argument:: subSamples
How many points the density is averaged over per bin. A centre sample, which is
code::subSamples: 1::, is wrong wherever the density is steep and hopeless where it
is singular: 1/x over a bin touching zero read 7000 against 3950 actually drawn.

METHOD:: deviation
The largest difference between counts and expectation, as a proportion of the
total. 0 is a perfect match; a few percent is visible in the picture.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: pdf
The density, as a Symbol or a Function.
METHOD:: expected
Expected counts per bin.
METHOD:: deviation
How far the samples sit from the density.
METHOD:: showExpected
METHOD:: pdfColorKey

EXAMPLES::
code::
// exprand is 1/x, not exponential
PltDistribution({ exprand(0.01, 1.0) } ! 20000, 0.01, 1, 40, \logUniform).front;

// and it is nothing like flat
PltDistribution({ exprand(0.01, 1.0) } ! 20000, 0.01, 1, 40, \uniform).front;

// rand is flat
PltDistribution({ 1.0.rand } ! 20000, 0, 1, 40, \uniform).front;

// the mean of three uniforms is nearly gaussian, with a third of the spread
(
PltDistribution({ ({ 1.0.rand } ! 3).sum / 3 } ! 20000, 0, 1, 40,
	PltDistribution.density(\gauss, [0.5, 0.167]), "mean of three").front;
)

// any function, unnormalised
PltDistribution({ 1.0.rand.squared } ! 20000, 0, 1, 40, { |x| 1 / x.sqrt }).front;

// data only
(
var counts = PlotLib.histogram({ exprand(0.01, 1.0) } ! 5000, 0.01, 1, 20);
var expected = PltDistribution.expectedCounts(
	PltDistribution.density(\logUniform), 0.01, 1, 20, 5000);
PltDistribution.deviation(counts, expected);
)
::
